  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 142 PM EDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nHigh pressure centered over IL/IN today will settle over the central \nAppalachians tonight, with a ridge extending south across the \nSouthern Appalachians. Clear, calm conditions tonight will favor fog \ndevelopment for mainly northern sections near bodies of water, much \nlike we saw this morning. Mostly sunny skies and relatively \ncomfortable temperatures/humidity for late July can be expected \nagain tomorrow, though highs will be a couple degrees warmer than \ntoday under a building midlevel ridge. \n\nDeep moisture starts to increase Saturday morning as a low pressure \nsystem tracks into the lower OH Valley, with PW values rising over 2 \ninches through the day. An initial round of showers/storms will come \nin the morning with the passage of a shortwave trough, with \nconvection likely to be elevated and sub-severe. A line of \nconvection forms in the afternoon in West TN along the cold front, \ncloser to the main upper trough axis and an upper jet streak. The \nNAM is quite vigorous in develping this convection as it crosses \nMiddle TN, but by the time it reaches our area (around midnight), it \nwill likely be in a much weaker state. Upper level forcing will be \nmostly to our north as the jet streak lifts into eastern KY/OH in \nthe evening. While there is some weak deep layer shear present, the \nnighttime arrival will limit surface-based instability. Joint \nprobability of MUCAPE > 500 J/kg and effective shear > 30 kt is \nunder 20% in our western half Saturday evening. Thus, the severe \nrisk appears low at this time. Flooding may be more of a concern if \nheavy rain occurs with the morning round, followed by additional \nheavy rain in the same areas during the evening/overnight round. \nModel event-total QPF amounts are not impressive (less than 1 inch \nin most locations), but localized heavy rain and flooding can't be \nruled out.\n\nA shortwave trough breaks off from the larger trough on Sunday and \nslowly tracks across the Deep South through Wednesday, keeping rain \nchances in the foreast. With the post-frontal surface air mass and \npersistent trough in the mid/upper levels, temperatures will be \nbelow normal for this period.",
